PART 1 -- HAND-DRIVEN SQL: which clause replaces, which one drops
==============================================================================
  READ IN SOURCE  langgraph/checkpoint/postgres/__init__.py:363-367
    query = (self.UPSERT_CHECKPOINT_WRITES_SQL       # DO UPDATE
             if all(w[0] in WRITES_IDX_MAP for w in writes)
             else self.INSERT_CHECKPOINT_WRITES_SQL) # DO NOTHING
  READ IN SOURCE  langgraph/checkpoint/base/__init__.py:795
    WRITES_IDX_MAP = {'__error__': -1, '__scheduled__': -2, '__interrupt__': -3, '__resume__': -4}
  READ IN SOURCE  postgres/base.py:146-159 -- both statements conflict on
    (thread_id, checkpoint_ns, checkpoint_id, task_id, idx); DO UPDATE SET
    lists channel, type, blob -- and NOT task_path.

  HAND-DRIVEN SQL  two writers, one conflict key, both real statements:
    INSERT .. ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING -> rows=1  blob='SURVIVOR'  task_path='path-of-survivor'
    UPSERT .. ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE  -> rows=1  blob='ZOMBIE'  task_path='path-of-survivor'
  [PASS] at one conflict key DO NOTHING keeps the FIRST writer's blob and DO UPDATE keeps the SECOND's  -- so the loser of a DO UPDATE collision is the live worker, not the stale one
    note: task_path is not in the SET list, so after a DO UPDATE the row carries
          the SECOND writer's blob under the FIRST writer's task_path. Since idx is
          derived from the channel for special writes, the reachable effect of the SET
          list is blob+type replacement at a fixed (channel, idx) -- not a channel swap.

  HAND-DRIVEN SQL  the same collision through the real put_writes() API,
  two savers on two connections, so the branch is chosen by langgraph:
    ALL channels special  [('__resume__', v)]  -> idx=[-4] __resume__='ZOMBIE'
    MIXED  [('__resume__', v), ('log', [v])]   -> idx=[-4, 1] __resume__='SURVIVOR'
  [PASS] put_writes ITSELF routes a pure-special batch to DO UPDATE (second writer wins) and a mixed batch to DO NOTHING (first writer wins)  -- the branch selection is measured, not inferred from reading the source

  HAND-DRIVEN SQL  the idx a channel lands on (the conflict key's last column):
    t-ordinary   idx=0   log
    t-ordinary   idx=1   other
    t-special    idx=-4  __resume__
    t-special    idx=-3  __interrupt__
    t-special    idx=-2  __scheduled__
    t-special    idx=-1  __error__
  [PASS] each special channel lands on its OWN fixed negative idx, so two workers writing the same control channel at one checkpoint always collide  -- {'__resume__': -4, '__interrupt__': -3, '__scheduled__': -2, '__error__': -1}

