PART 1b -- REAL GRAPH RUN: which batches actually take the DO UPDATE path
==============================================================================
  one worker, one thread: start -> interrupt -> resume -> interrupt -> resume -> done
  every put_writes batch it issued:
    cp ...d856abe2  06a207e3     ['log', 'branch:to:approve']                   -> INSERT/DO NOTHING
    cp ...f10c7c0f  f7fe1f19     ['__interrupt__']                              -> UPSERT/DO UPDATE
    cp ...f10c7c0f  NULL_TASK_ID ['__resume__']                                 -> UPSERT/DO UPDATE
    cp ...f10c7c0f  f7fe1f19     ['__interrupt__', '__resume__']                -> UPSERT/DO UPDATE
    cp ...f10c7c0f  NULL_TASK_ID ['__resume__']                                 -> UPSERT/DO UPDATE
    cp ...f10c7c0f  f7fe1f19     ['__resume__', '__resume__', 'log']            -> INSERT/DO NOTHING
  [PASS] a real graph run DOES route batches to the DO UPDATE statement  -- 4 of 6 batches: ['__interrupt__']; ['__resume__']; ['__interrupt__', '__resume__']; ['__resume__']
  final state: log=['start', 'R1|R2']
  READ IN SOURCE, matching the three shapes above:
    pregel/_loop.py:919-926  Command(resume=v) -> put_writes(NULL_TASK_ID, [(RESUME, v)])
    pregel/_runner.py:585-591  GraphInterrupt -> put_writes(task_id, [(INTERRUPT, ..)] + RESUME)
    pregel/_runner.py:596-604  an unhandled node error with no prior channel write
      -> put_writes(task_id, [(ERROR, exc)]) -- also all-special, also DO UPDATE (not exercised here)

