==============================================================================
PART 2b -- REAL GRAPH RUN, NO INSTRUMENTATION: the collision without a gate
==============================================================================
  Part 2's gate only decides WHICH worker writes last. Here: two processes
  spawned together (alternating which goes first), no gate, no SIGSTOP, no
  fence -- so the winner is decided by scheduling alone. The tally below
  varies from run to run and is therefore reported, not asserted; the one
  thing that is not a matter of timing IS asserted -- that the two
  processes contend for ONE row rather than storing one each.
    trial 0  spawned A then B  rows at (NULL_TASK_ID,-4)=1  kept=['A-VALUE']
             A:+0.000->+0.019s/log=['start']  B:+0.000->+0.019s/log=['start']
             then a later worker resumed with 'SECOND' -> ['start', 'A-VALUE|SECOND']
    trial 1  spawned B then A  rows at (NULL_TASK_ID,-4)=1  kept=['A-VALUE']
             A:+0.000->+0.018s/log=['start']  B:+0.000->+0.018s/log=['start']
             then a later worker resumed with 'SECOND' -> ['start', 'A-VALUE|SECOND']
    trial 2  spawned A then B  rows at (NULL_TASK_ID,-4)=1  kept=['A-VALUE']
             A:+0.000->+0.014s/log=['start']  B:+0.000->+0.014s/log=['start']
             then a later worker resumed with 'SECOND' -> ['start', 'A-VALUE|SECOND']
    trial 3  spawned B then A  rows at (NULL_TASK_ID,-4)=1  kept=['A-VALUE']
             A:+0.000->+0.012s/log=['start']  B:+0.000->+0.012s/log=['start']
             then a later worker resumed with 'SECOND' -> ['start', 'A-VALUE|SECOND']
    trial 4  spawned A then B  rows at (NULL_TASK_ID,-4)=1  kept=['A-VALUE']
             A:+0.000->+0.010s/log=['start']  B:+0.000->+0.010s/log=['start']
             then a later worker resumed with 'SECOND' -> ['start', 'A-VALUE|SECOND']
  which worker's value the thread kept, over 5 trials: {"['A-VALUE']": 5}
  [PASS] two unsynchronised processes always contend for ONE row at the shared conflict key -- they never store one each  -- so the collision needs no instrumentation; the gate in Part 2 only makes the winner deterministic

