==============================================================================
VERDICT: PROVEN
  SQL      a write whose channels are ALL in WRITES_IDX_MAP takes
           UPSERT .. ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE, so the SECOND writer at a
           conflict key wins; an ordinary or mixed write at the same key
           takes DO NOTHING and the second writer is dropped. Measured
           through put_writes() itself, not inferred from the source.
  OFF      two OS processes on one thread_id: the SIGSTOP'd worker's
           __resume__ write REPLACED the live worker's, at the same
           checkpoint, with no fork and no added checkpoint -- so this is
           a different mechanism from the dropped write in proof.py. A
           later worker then resumed the run and finished it on a human
           input that was never given to it. All three workers exit 0.
  ON       proof/fence.py already blocks it: the guard sits at
           _cursor(pipeline=True), upstream of the statement choice, so
           the zombie is refused by Postgres (SQLSTATE 22012) on the DO
           UPDATE path exactly as on DO NOTHING, and the later worker
           finishes on the input actually supplied.
